1
00:00:24,213 --> 00:00:30,241
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31,063 --> 00:00:36,430
We are going to continue our discussion on VR Navigation Experience Optimization.

3
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:44,902
In other words, we are trying to understand in these lectures how to optimize the navigation experience in VR.

4
00:00:47,506 --> 00:00:53,494
So, so far we have discussed the idea of

5
00:00:54,233 --> 00:01:06,294
the psychophysiological or experience optimization in VR, where we emphasize that the objective is to primarily take care of the cyber sickness related issues,

6
00:01:06,294 --> 00:01:14,361
how we can take measures to avoid the onset of cyber sickness or to minimize the possibility of cyber sickness.

7
00:01:15,322 --> 00:01:22,232
Now, in order to do that, we talked about one realistic natural walking model.

8
00:01:23,258 --> 00:01:30,987
And we have seen how this model can be utilized to formulate two approaches.

9
00:01:31,107 --> 00:01:37,654
One is the optimal path approach, the other one is the optimal path under time constraint approach.

10
00:01:37,674 --> 00:01:52,911
In the optimal path approach, we talked about identifying a path in this virtual world that lets the user explore all points of interest,

11
00:01:53,937 --> 00:01:54,918
in minimum time.

12
00:01:57,803 --> 00:02:05,186
Now, the problem with this approach is that if the virtual world is large, such as the Majuli River Island,

13
00:02:05,186 --> 00:02:15,709
this virtual tour, then there may be a large number of points of interest, and to cover all the points of interest,

14
00:02:17,866 --> 00:02:25,010
to find out a path that covers all the points of interest in minimum time may not solve our issue because that minimum time,

15
00:02:25,010 --> 00:02:43,030
the minimum duration to cover, assuming natural walking speed, may still be very large as compared to the duration we expect to let the user be exposed to in the virtual world,

16
00:02:47,262 --> 00:02:49,685
which can induce cyber sickness.

17
00:02:50,206 --> 00:02:57,995
So, essentially this optimal path solution may still lead to cyber sickness if the virtual world is very large.

18
00:02:58,456 --> 00:03:03,722
So, in such cases, we are assuming that the user is exploring the world by walking.

19
00:03:06,866 --> 00:03:14,015
In such a case, we can go for another approach, that is, optimal path under a time constraint.

20
00:03:14,115 --> 00:03:16,999
So, there is an upper limit of time.

21
00:03:18,042 --> 00:03:25,473
So, the user will be exposed to the virtual tour at most for that time duration, which we are calling the time threshold.

22
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:42,700
And within that time duration, the user is expected to cover a path that will allow the user to cover the maximum number of the most important points of interest, where by the term 'covering'

23
00:03:43,473 --> 00:03:50,260
I also mean that the user should be able to interact with or inspect those, along with navigation from one point to another.

24
00:03:51,261 --> 00:04:02,833
So, in order to do that, we introduced a few more terms, such as the importance of a node, waiting time at a node, and then we showed how to solve this problem.

25
00:04:04,615 --> 00:04:09,160
So, these two we have discussed in the previous parts of the lecture.

26
00:04:11,857 --> 00:04:17,562
Now, in this lecture, we will try to understand how this theory can be put into practice.

27
00:04:18,944 --> 00:04:22,781
Of course, we will not go into all the details of the theory, but

28
00:04:22,781 --> 00:04:35,359
the initial part, that is, how a practical problem can be modeled in this graph theoretic form so that these optimization approaches can be applied.

29
00:04:37,281 --> 00:04:39,163
That we will do in terms of the

30
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,102
case study that we introduced earlier.

31
00:04:44,665 --> 00:04:51,031
So let us try to understand how we can use the idea in practice with respect to the Majuli case study.

32
00:04:52,412 --> 00:05:08,087
Just to recap, Majuli is the river island on the Brahmaputra River, which is in the eastern part of the country in the state of Assam, and this is how the river island looks, surrounded by

33
00:05:08,827 --> 00:05:13,652
rivers on all sides by the Brahmaputra River and its tributaries.

34
00:05:17,416 --> 00:05:26,025
It is also the world's largest inhabited river island and a contender for UNESCO World Heritage Site status.

35
00:05:26,045 --> 00:05:38,378
So, there are these Satras, or the monasteries of the Vaisnavite faith, which contain several cultural aspects,

36
00:05:39,472 --> 00:05:41,735
both tangible and intangible.

37
00:05:42,776 --> 00:05:47,302
And there is a total of 22 Satras.

38
00:05:49,004 --> 00:05:58,112
Each Satra has several parts, including a Naamghar, the prayer hall, which is the central part, then the Monikut,

39
00:05:58,112 --> 00:06:05,985
the place of the idol, Haatis, the hostel of the disciples, and residences of the satradhikar or the master of the Satra.

40
00:06:08,328 --> 00:06:18,503
On that island, there is one Auniati Satra, which is one of the largest, most prominent, and most visited Satras on the island.

41
00:06:21,908 --> 00:06:29,278
Now, the campus of that Satra covers approximately 1200 square meters, a pretty large area.

42
00:06:31,341 --> 00:06:37,390
And we have estimated that there are at least 25 points of interest

43
00:06:38,686 --> 00:06:40,768
in that Satra, at least.

44
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:53,740
Now, what we want is to make a system-controlled VR tour to cover the Satra to get the experience of that Satra.

45
00:06:55,882 --> 00:07:06,752
Here we are assuming that we will be having a walking-based exploration, and in the walking-based exploration, to have a realistic

46
00:07:07,390 --> 00:07:15,741
Experience will be using the natural walking speed model that we had discussed earlier.

47
00:07:18,024 --> 00:07:35,608
Now, in a practical scenario, if somebody wants to explore the whole Satra by walking, it may take up to a day to explore the whole site, including stoppage at the points of interest. Clearly,

48
00:07:36,128 --> 00:07:48,399
the whole day exposure to a virtual world, if we are trying to do the same in the virtual tour, is not recommended for a VR tour because that is surely going to induce cyber sickness.

49
00:07:48,419 --> 00:08:03,773
So, we should not do it, but then how can we come up with an optimum path that minimizes this duration? And if the minimum duration is still not at an acceptable level,

50
00:08:05,002 --> 00:08:20,483
still not below the time threshold, then how can we come up with a path that allows the user to explore the most important POIs in the virtual world within the given time threshold?

51
00:08:24,709 --> 00:08:33,220
So, using either of the approaches, we can actually propose a path to maximize the user experience in the virtual world.

52
00:08:33,773 --> 00:08:35,796
Exploration of the Satra.

53
00:08:38,938 --> 00:08:42,864
In order to do that, first we need to map the site to a graph.

54
00:08:42,884 --> 00:08:54,158
So, essentially we need to convert the Satra, the campus of the Satra with all its POIs, into a graph where the points of interest will be the nodes.

55
00:08:54,178 --> 00:08:58,103
So, as I said before, at least 25 points of interest are there.

56
00:08:58,123 --> 00:09:00,386
So, at least 25 nodes should be there.

57
00:09:02,048 --> 00:09:02,869
Also, we need to

58
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:15,831
calculate the distance between them, the Euclidean distance, so that we can use it as the edge weights, edges that are connecting, edges that are connecting pairs of nodes.

59
00:09:18,974 --> 00:09:29,243
Also, if we want to implement the second approach, assuming the first approach yields a minimum time which is still more than the time threshold,

60
00:09:29,243 --> 00:09:34,189
then we need to have some idea of the relative importance of each of the

61
00:09:34,456 --> 00:09:40,283
points of interest or each of the nodes.

62
00:09:40,303 --> 00:09:55,462
Along with that, in order to be able to find out an optimal path following the second approach, we also need to estimate the maximum wait time at each POI, which is required

63
00:09:55,462 --> 00:10:02,792
for a user to stop at the POI, inspect it, interact with it if possible, and then resume the navigation.

64
00:10:07,919 --> 00:10:11,744
So there are several challenges, several things to figure out.

65
00:10:14,547 --> 00:10:19,614
The first thing is converting the site to a graph representation.

66
00:10:20,535 --> 00:10:22,057
How do we do the mapping?

67
00:10:22,778 --> 00:10:31,889
Clearly, this is not an easy task to do, and it requires detailed field study and, of course, patience because it cannot be done in one shot.

68
00:10:31,909 --> 00:10:36,475
So initially, you can come up with a plan of converting it to a graph, but then that graph may not look

69
00:10:36,623 --> 00:10:38,185
proper; you need to revise it.

70
00:10:39,147 --> 00:10:40,188
It is an iterative process.

71
00:10:41,530 --> 00:10:45,877
It requires patience.

72
00:10:45,897 --> 00:10:49,662
Now, in our case, what did we do?

73
00:10:54,269 --> 00:10:56,553
A team visited the site.

74
00:10:56,573 --> 00:11:06,107
So, one field trip was made during the Kati Bihu festival, which gives a better idea about the places of importance, the POIs,

75
00:11:07,943 --> 00:11:12,069
the observation and interaction time, or the waiting time at each POI.

76
00:11:17,817 --> 00:11:27,291
Any festival time could have been picked, but due to several logistical issues, we had chosen one of those important festival times.

77
00:11:29,734 --> 00:11:36,364
Now, observed during that field visit, the team observed the people's travel behavior.

78
00:11:37,289 --> 00:11:45,057
Throughout the whole day, how the people travel within this Satra, where they stop, what they do, it is a kind of field study.

79
00:11:48,541 --> 00:11:52,905
Or, in other words, you can say it is a kind of contextual inquiry.

80
00:11:55,368 --> 00:12:03,516
Similar to a contextual inquiry, so you can refer to earlier lectures on contextual inquiry to understand this concept.

81
00:12:05,318 --> 00:12:19,192
Now, the team also talked to local people, mostly campus residents and the Satra committee members, for further clarity on certain aspects of the observations.

82
00:12:19,212 --> 00:12:23,837
Now, the team members asked two questions to the people.

83
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:30,163
What are typically the places people visit when they come to this place?

84
00:12:31,445 --> 00:12:34,768
And what are the places you, that means the

85
00:12:35,153 --> 00:12:41,199
person being interviewed, suggest a tourist must visit when they come to the Satra.

86
00:12:44,281 --> 00:12:56,292
Now, based on observations, manual recording of the behavior, and interactions with the committee members or people who are residents of the campus,

87
00:12:56,292 --> 00:13:03,439
the team could accumulate the desired information in terms of the points of interest and then

88
00:13:05,377 --> 00:13:08,581
could formulate a graph.

89
00:13:08,601 --> 00:13:11,245
So, they could accumulate information on points of interest.

90
00:13:12,647 --> 00:13:17,513
Once the points of interest are known, then the distance between them can be measured, which they did.

91
00:13:18,514 --> 00:13:23,981
And then that can be used as weight between the nodes.

92
00:13:25,744 --> 00:13:31,992
Also, by talking to the people, some idea was

93
00:13:32,393 --> 00:13:40,144
gained about the relative importance of each node, and accordingly the importance was assigned to the nodes.

94
00:13:42,428 --> 00:13:53,484
And also, based on the observation of people's behavior when they visit the places, an estimate of the maximum wait time was done at each POI.

95
00:13:54,846 --> 00:13:55,667
So, all these

96
00:13:56,018 --> 00:14:06,672
information was obtained through observations, manual recording, and interactions with the people there.

97
00:14:06,692 --> 00:14:22,954
So, based on that analysis of data and the information, the campus can be modeled in this form, having the points of interest

98
00:14:26,984 --> 00:14:34,716
having some locations, places, objects, and the points of interest.

99
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:44,010
So, as you can see here, the nodes in circles indicate the points of interest.

100
00:14:45,232 --> 00:14:49,178
Note that the points of interest need not always

101
00:14:49,647 --> 00:14:55,115
refer to the actual physical object; such as a lake is not considered a point of interest.

102
00:14:55,135 --> 00:14:59,321
However, the corners of the lake are considered points of interest.

103
00:15:01,484 --> 00:15:17,848
So, starting from the entry point number 1, then the gate is another point of interest, then different paths, and along the paths, different points of interest are marked.

104
00:15:18,436 --> 00:15:37,425
So, in this graph, you can see the points of interest denoted by these filled circles, whereas the artifacts and places in the campus are shown in rectangular forms.

105
00:15:39,488 --> 00:15:47,160
So, this is what was obtained based on contextual inquiry or the field study.

106
00:15:47,798 --> 00:16:05,280
And once we know the points of interest and have formulated a graph, say for example, between node 12 and 13, the distance was measured as 58 meters.

107
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:16,033
So, this will serve as the weight between these nodes or the weight for this edge.

108
00:16:17,785 --> 00:16:29,983
Similarly, everywhere the weight was obtained, and also the relative importance was given plus wait time.

109
00:16:30,584 --> 00:16:33,849
So, that relative importance and wait time is not shown here.

110
00:16:34,970 --> 00:16:40,739
To keep things simple, only the graph form is shown along with the distance between the nodes.

111
00:16:41,700 --> 00:16:46,307
Now, once this is there, we can readily use our algorithms to either

112
00:16:46,979 --> 00:16:58,385
come up with an optimal path covering all the nodes, 25 nodes which can be navigated,

113
00:16:58,385 --> 00:17:09,492
which can be traversed in minimum time, or we can come up with an optimal path which can be traversed within a time threshold.

114
00:17:10,034 --> 00:17:15,061
But during that period, the maximum number of most important POIs can be covered,

115
00:17:15,481 --> 00:17:18,765
taking into account the wait time at each POI.

116
00:17:21,628 --> 00:17:27,297
So, that can be done straightforwardly from here based on the algorithms and the available tools.

117
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:35,488
So, this is how we can put the idea into practice.

118
00:17:35,508 --> 00:17:45,201
Thus, once this graph is obtained, we can now apply the algorithms to get an optimal path using a TSP solver,

119
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:57,313
the whole path covering all the nodes, or an optimal path within the given time constant set by us, that is the time threshold, using the VRPP solver.

120
00:18:00,777 --> 00:18:06,604
So, let us quickly summarize what we have done so far.

121
00:18:09,748 --> 00:18:16,015
So, we started our discussion on experience optimization in XR. Specifically, we are talking about

122
00:18:16,552 --> 00:18:23,199
VR experience optimization, where we are emphasizing that this is related to psychophysiological optimization.

123
00:18:23,219 --> 00:18:35,343
So, we talked about the idea of optimization, then the idea of psychophysiological optimization in VR experience, which was primarily aimed

124
00:18:35,343 --> 00:18:44,162
to take care of the cybersickness issues. There, we talked about a couple of approaches: optimal path, optimal path under time constant, and we have also seen

125
00:18:45,593 --> 00:18:52,001
how these approaches can be put into practice.

126
00:18:52,021 --> 00:18:58,409
So, we talked about two types of optimizations that are of importance to XR.

127
00:18:58,769 --> 00:19:04,096
One is psychophysiological or experience optimization, and the other one is resource optimization.

128
00:19:04,116 --> 00:19:15,550
So, in the next lecture, our focus will be on resource optimization for immersive experience.

129
00:19:16,610 --> 00:19:21,876
Throughout the discussion, we referred to many prior works.

130
00:19:22,797 --> 00:19:31,026
So, this is the list of references that can be used to get more ideas on the different topics that are covered.

131
00:19:33,009 --> 00:19:37,434
So, references 1, 2, and 3 are there.

132
00:19:38,355 --> 00:19:40,938
Along with that, there are three more references: 4, 5, and 6.

133
00:19:40,978 --> 00:19:44,862
So, you can make use of these six references

134
00:19:47,423 --> 00:19:50,989
to get more clarity on some of the topics that we have discussed here.

135
00:19:52,692 --> 00:20:02,790
So, I hope you could follow the overall flow of the lecture, the concepts covered, the topics discussed, and that you enjoyed the discussion.

136
00:20:03,372 --> 00:20:07,819
So, looking forward to meeting you again in the next lecture on resource optimization.

137
00:20:08,601 --> 00:20:12,047
So, until then, thank you, goodbye, and happy learning.
